{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And by your sword you shall live.</b> According to his craft, for he was a man of the field, hunting in wildernesses and in places of desolation and ruin, he was suited to the blessing of the sword. And Mars, which governs those who shed blood, has its power great over desolation and the sword, and therefore the seed of Esav inherits the sword; and his prince, which is the killing force, is assigned the sword from of old. And for this reason the Torah withheld the sword and iron from the Tabernacle and the Temple: concerning the Tabernacle it is written (Exodus 25:3) ״זהב וכסף ונחשת״ — gold, silver, and copper — and there was no iron there at all; and concerning the Temple it is written (I Kings 6:7) ״ומקבות והגרזן כל כלי ברזל לא נשמע בבית בהבנותו״ — neither hammer nor axe nor any tool of iron was heard in the House while it was being built — because iron is the sword, and it is what destroys the world, while the Temple is the maintenance of the world." "<b>And your brother you shall serve.</b> When Yaakov is worthy. <b>And it shall come to pass, when you grow restless.</b> When he is not worthy. <b>And you shall break his yoke from your neck.</b> And this is the intent of Onkelos, who translated: ״כד יעברון בנוהי על פתגמי אורייתא״ — when they transgress the words of the Torah."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:41.355282004Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}